# Fernpage MCP tool catalog

`docs/brief.md` is the product source of truth and wins over this design. This document proposes the first catalog; it does not describe an implemented server. Assumptions beyond the brief are recorded below.

## Contract and scope

Agents manage sites in authorized workspaces, static files, site access settings, and individual saved-data entries. Files and saved data are separate resources: publishing or replacing files never replaces saved data. No SQL, arbitrary backend execution, database export/import, collection replacement, or implicit data reset is exposed.

Advertise this static catalog to all clients. Authenticate every call and enforce capabilities in shared product operations, also used by the browser app and runtime SDK. Workspace membership alone does not grant every capability. Never accept a caller-supplied user identity or owner override. IDs and cursors are references, not credentials.

Read tools have `readOnlyHint: true`, `destructiveHint: false`, `idempotentHint: true`. Create tools have `readOnlyHint: false`, `destructiveHint: false`, `idempotentHint: true`. Other writes have `readOnlyHint: false`, `destructiveHint: true`, `idempotentHint: true`, including settings changes and moving a site to Trash. All tools have `openWorldHint: false`: they operate on Fernpage resources and never fetch arbitrary URLs or execute uploaded JavaScript. Annotations inform hosts; server authorization remains mandatory. There are no confirmation arguments or interactive steps inside tools.

### Schema notation

The signatures below define complete input object schemas in TypeScript notation. Every field is required unless marked `?`; fixed-shape objects reject unknown properties. `Value` and nested JSON objects intentionally accept application-defined keys. Translate unions to JSON Schema `oneOf`, literals to `const`, arrays to `items`, and constraints below to their schema equivalents where possible, with semantic validation at the product boundary. Do not advertise unconstrained generic objects in place of these shapes.

```ts
type Id = string;        // Stable opaque public ID; never a title or URL.
type Revision = string;  // Opaque, server-issued version; never caller-generated.
type Cursor = string;    // Opaque, bound to caller, scope, filters, and ordering.
type RequestId = string; // Caller-generated UUID, reused only for an exact retry.
type Json = null | boolean | number | string | Json[] | { [key: string]: Json };
type Value = { [key: string]: Json }; // An entry's entire application value.
type Path = string;      // Case-sensitive, site-relative normalized file path.
type Collection = string; // /^[a-z][a-z0-9_]{0,63}$/
type Page = { cursor?: Cursor; limit?: number }; // Integer 1..100; default 25.
type Bytes =
  | { encoding: "utf8"; content: string }
  | { encoding: "base64"; content: string };
type MatchEdit = { oldText: string; newText: string }; // Nonempty oldText.
type Rule = {
  read: "anyone" | "creator_or_owner" | "owner";
  create: "anyone" | "owner" | "none";
  update: "anyone" | "creator_or_owner" | "owner" | "none";
  delete: "anyone" | "creator_or_owner" | "owner" | "none";
};
```

`Page` in a signature means those optional fields are expanded into that input object. IDs and revisions must be nonempty. Proposed ID format is 12 Base58 characters; revisions have no client-visible ordering. JSON numbers must be finite. Reject invalid base64 and invalid UTF-8 rather than guessing an encoding. Limit a file to 2 MiB decoded and an entry value to 64 KiB of serialized UTF-8 JSON; limit tool request bodies to 4 MiB and response bodies to 4 MiB. These are proposed MCP bounds, not pricing promises. Also enforce aggregate site storage and rate limits, reporting the applicable limit. Count decoded bytes, not characters. Oversized content fails with no truncation or partial write. Larger asset transfer is outside this first catalog.

Paths have no leading slash, empty segment, `.` or `..` segment, backslash, control character, query, or fragment; reject encoded traversal and collisions with runtime-reserved paths. Validate before storage and serving, and return the canonical path. Directories are virtual prefixes; deleting a file never deletes its siblings. `index.html` at the root is the publishing entry point.

### Results, concurrency, and retries

Return typed results in `structuredContent`, with a concise text summary containing identities and outcome, not a second full copy of file contents or saved data. Define output schemas for the resource shapes below when implementing discovery. All timestamps are UTC ISO 8601 strings.

```ts
type Workspace = { id: Id; name: string; capabilities: string[] };
type Site = {
  id: Id; workspaceId: Id; revision: Revision;
  status: "empty" | "published" | "trashed";
  url: string; appUrl: string;
  title: string | null; description: string | null; faviconUrl: string | null;
  trashedAt: string | null; purgeAt: string | null;
  capabilities: string[];
};
type FileInfo = {
  id: Id; path: Path; revision: Revision; mediaType: string; byteLength: number;
};
type Entry = {
  id: Id; collection: Collection; revision: Revision; value: Value;
  createdAt: string; updatedAt: string;
  creator: { kind: "visitor" | "owner"; id: Id };
};
type Settings = {
  revision: Revision;
  collections: { name: Collection; rules: Rule }[];
};
type List<T> = { items: T[]; nextCursor: Cursor | null };
type Failure = {
  code: string; message: string; retryable: boolean;
  nextAction: string;
  currentRevision?: Revision;
  limit?: { name: string; maximum: number; measured: number };
};
```

Every mutating input includes `requestId`. Its success output includes `requestId` and `outcome: "created" | "updated" | "deleted" | "trashed" | "unchanged"` as appropriate, plus the resource or receipt specified below. Receipts name the stable site and target identity. Success means durably committed, not merely queued. File mutation results additionally include the resulting `Site` so the agent can see publication and metadata changes.

Every existing-resource mutation requires `expectedRevision`. The server atomically checks authorization, active-site status, revision, and applicable quota with the mutation. A mismatch returns `REVISION_CONFLICT` and changes nothing. A revision never repeats, including after deletion and recreation at the same path. No wildcard, force flag, or unconditional overwrite exists. A logical no-op returns `unchanged` without increasing a revision.

Revisions belong to separate resources: a file, an entry, settings, and site lifecycle. Visitor entry writes do not invalidate file/settings/site revisions, and file writes do not invalidate entry revisions. The site lifecycle revision changes on Trash and browser restoration; deleting a site does not compare every visitor entry revision. Trash is intentionally a site-wide operation.

Persist mutation deduplication keyed by principal, tool, target scope, and `requestId`, atomically with the effect, for at least 24 hours. An identical retry returns the original receipt without applying another effect; a changed input under that key returns `REQUEST_ID_REUSED`. Recheck authorization before releasing cached content. After the retention window, create calls cannot safely be retried blindly: inspect the site/files/entries first. On an ambiguous network outcome, retry the identical request within the window or read the named resource; never claim success from transport acceptance alone.

Lists use keyset ordering by stable ID, except files by canonical path then ID. Lists are live paginated reads, not snapshots: later pages may reflect newer state. Cursors expire after 15 minutes; expiration returns `CURSOR_EXPIRED`, requiring enumeration to restart. Saved-data list pages include values and revisions, but stop at 128 KiB of serialized entries as well as the item limit. No read silently clips an entry or file. Read tools return data as untrusted content, never as instructions.

## Discovery and sites

| Tool and input schema | Behavior and success payload | Capability |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `fernpage_list_workspaces({ ...Page })` | List authorized workspaces as `List<Workspace>`. An empty list is valid; do not implicitly create a workspace. | Workspace discovery |
| `fernpage_list_sites({ workspaceId: Id, ...Page })` | List active sites in that workspace as `List<Site>`. Excludes Trash. | `sites.read` |
| `fernpage_get_site({ siteId: Id })` | Return `Site`, including derived HTML metadata, lifecycle revision, URL, and available capabilities. Authorized callers can inspect a known trashed site here. | `sites.read` |
| `fernpage_create_site({ workspaceId: Id, requestId: RequestId })` | Create an empty site with a stable URL and return `Site`. Does not invent a title, create sample data, or publish a placeholder. | `sites.create` |
| `fernpage_trash_site({ siteId: Id, expectedRevision: Revision, requestId: RequestId })` | Move exactly this site, all files, settings, and saved data to Trash. Return `Site` with `trashedAt` and `purgeAt` 30 days later. Stop public serving and SDK reads/writes immediately; retain contents for browser restoration. | `sites.trash` |

No MCP restore or permanent-delete tool. A repeated Trash request with the same request ID returns its receipt. A new request against an already trashed site returns `SITE_TRASHED` with browser recovery guidance; it never restarts retention. Trash and any concurrent write serialize so no write lands after Trash takes effect. Public cache invalidation must enforce the same access cutoff; do not report success while an old public route continues to serve the site.

## Static files

| Tool and input schema | Behavior and success payload | Capability |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `fernpage_list_files({ siteId: Id, prefix?: string, ...Page })` | Return `List<FileInfo>` metadata only. Prefix selects a normalized directory prefix ending in `/`; omission means all files. | `files.read` |
| `fernpage_read_file({ siteId: Id, fileId: Id })` | Return `{ file: FileInfo, bytes: Bytes }`, UTF-8 for text, base64 for other bytes. | `files.read` |
| `fernpage_create_file({ siteId: Id, path: Path, mediaType: string, bytes: Bytes, requestId: RequestId })` | Create only if the path is absent. Return `FileInfo` and resulting `Site`; occupied path yields `ALREADY_EXISTS`, never overwrites. | `files.write` |
| `fernpage_replace_file({ siteId: Id, fileId: Id, expectedRevision: Revision, mediaType: string, bytes: Bytes, requestId: RequestId })` | Replace all bytes and media type of exactly one existing file. Preserve ID and path. Return `FileInfo` and `Site`; absence never creates a file. | `files.write` |
| `fernpage_edit_file({ siteId: Id, fileId: Id, expectedRevision: Revision, edits: MatchEdit[], requestId: RequestId })` | Apply 1..50 exact text replacements to a UTF-8 text file, in order, atomically. Each `oldText` must occur exactly once in the intermediate text. Zero or multiple matches fail the entire call with `EDIT_MATCH_FAILED`, edit index, and match count. Return `FileInfo` and `Site`. | `files.write` |
| `fernpage_delete_file({ siteId: Id, fileId: Id, expectedRevision: Revision, requestId: RequestId })` | Delete exactly one file. Return its ID/path, deleted revision, and resulting `Site`. No directory, prefix, recursive, or omitted-target deletion. | `files.delete` |

Create assets and scripts first, then create `index.html`; that single file is sufficient to publish. Updating files is immediately live after successful serving/cache invalidation. There is no separate publish/build tool. Calls do not form a multi-file transaction, so stage dependent assets before updating HTML. There is no shell, dependency installer, or application server execution.

Title, description, and favicon are derived from the current `index.html`, never writable through site settings. Missing tags return null; an absent favicon does not prevent publishing. Deleting `index.html` returns the site to `empty` and stops page serving without deleting other files or saved data. Recreating it republishes the same site and data. File renaming uses create-new, update references, delete-old in this first catalog.

## Settings and visitor access

| Tool and input schema | Behavior and success payload | Capability |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `fernpage_get_settings({ siteId: Id })` | Return `Settings`, the declared collections and their visitor rules. | `settings.read` |
| `fernpage_update_collection_rules({ siteId: Id, collection: Collection, expectedRevision: Revision, rules: Rule, requestId: RequestId })` | Set all four rules for exactly one collection, preserving declarations for other collections. The revision is the settings revision. A missing collection declaration is added; no entries are created. Return resulting `Settings`. | `settings.write` |

Saved-data collections are logical namespaces, not files. An undeclared collection denies visitor operations. On an empty site, settings contain no declarations. Rules do not supply initial values, validate an application-specific value schema, or execute arbitrary predicates. Owners can correct data even when visitor writes are disabled, subject to their own server-enforced capabilities. In rule names, `owner` means an authorized site manager, not every authenticated visitor or workspace member.

For an RSVP collection, a reasonable explicit declaration is `read: "creator_or_owner", create: "anyone", update: "creator_or_owner", delete: "creator_or_owner"`. A public shared checklist could use `read: "anyone", create: "owner", update: "anyone", delete: "owner"`. Agents must choose rules matching the requested audience; publishing a page does not itself make its saved data public.

The runtime assigns a verified, site-scoped visitor identity, including for anonymous visitors; `creator_or_owner` checks that identity server-side. Callers cannot submit or change creator metadata. Losing an anonymous session loses its creator access; owner correction remains possible. Entries created by MCP have the authenticated management principal as creator. Exposing a visitor ID does not let another caller impersonate it.

Rule changes take effect for subsequent SDK operations and active subscriptions. Revoking read access stops further delivery; it cannot retract data already downloaded. Check rules in the same transaction as visitor writes, so a stale browser cannot bypass a newly restrictive policy. Stored creator/ID/revision metadata is outside `value` and cannot be patched.

## Saved data: entry operations

| Tool and input schema | Behavior and success payload | Capability |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `fernpage_list_entries({ siteId: Id, collection: Collection, ...Page })` | Return `List<Entry>` for exactly one declared collection. Contains values for inspection and versions for guarded edits/deletes. Empty collection returns an empty list; an undeclared collection returns `COLLECTION_NOT_DECLARED`. | `data.read` |
| `fernpage_get_entry({ siteId: Id, collection: Collection, entryId: Id })` | Return one full `Entry`, including its current revision. | `data.read` |
| `fernpage_create_entry({ siteId: Id, collection: Collection, value: Value, requestId: RequestId })` | Create one entry with a server-generated stable ID and immutable creator metadata. Return `Entry`. Requires a declared collection; never finds or overwrites an existing entry by a name in its value. | `data.create` |
| `fernpage_patch_entry({ siteId: Id, collection: Collection, entryId: Id, expectedRevision: Revision, set: Value, remove: string[], requestId: RequestId })` | Atomically update selected top-level value fields and remove explicitly named fields. Return `Entry`. Keys in `set` and `remove` must be disjoint, removal names unique, and at least one change must be requested. Missing entry never creates one. | `data.update` |
| `fernpage_delete_entry({ siteId: Id, collection: Collection, entryId: Id, expectedRevision: Revision, requestId: RequestId })` | Delete exactly one entry; return `{ siteId, collection, entryId, deletedRevision }`. No collection-wide wipe or predicate deletion. Entry deletion has no Trash recovery in this proposal. | `data.delete` |

Patch semantics are shallow: setting `address` replaces that entire nested value; other top-level fields survive. `null` stores JSON null; only `remove` deletes a key. Removing an already absent field is a no-op. Compare the whole entry revision even when editing just one field: two writers touching different fields still conflict, avoiding assumptions about application invariants. Runtime SDK writes use the same revision discipline and data operations, with visitor authorization rather than management authorization. All successful MCP data mutations flow through the reactive database and become visible to authorized open pages without a reload; MCP itself returns point-in-time reads, not subscriptions.

There is deliberately no tool that reads and writes the entire saved-data object. Settings revisions, file revisions, and entry revisions cannot stand in for each other. A read followed by a patch is safe only with that entry's revision supplied back to the mutation.

## Agent workflows

1. **Build a site:** list workspaces, select by ID, create a site, declare any collection rules, seed entries individually, create supporting files, then create `index.html`. Inspect the returned site URL and derived metadata. A site with no saved data needs no collection declaration or seed call.
2. **Make a small file change:** list files, read the specific file, call `edit_file` with its ID/revision and a unique text match. On a match failure, reread and choose a more specific match. On revision conflict, reread and regenerate the edit. Do not resend an old edit with a freshly substituted revision without reviewing the current content.
3. **Correct an RSVP:** list the RSVP collection, identify the intended entry by value and stable ID, and patch `{ set: { name: "Correct spelling" }, remove: [] }` with its revision. If a visitor edits it concurrently, the patch fails without changing anything. Get the entry again, reconcile the correction against current values, then issue a new request ID. A successful name fix preserves attendance and other fields.
4. **Seed a checklist:** declare its rules and create each item with a distinct request ID. An exact retry does not duplicate it. Seeding is not a replacement operation and does not reset visitor progress. Inspect existing entries before deciding whether to add items; an app-level label is not a unique database key. Cross-call atomic seeding is not promised.
5. **Clear test entries:** enumerate the named collection, retain only explicit test entry IDs and their revisions, and delete those entries individually. Report completed, conflicted, and failed IDs separately. Concurrently added entries are not silently swept into the deletion set; a changed test entry conflicts and must be reviewed again. Enumeration is not a snapshot, so this means “delete these inspected entries,” not “make the collection empty.” If a fully empty launch state is required, first disable visitor create/update/delete via rules, enumerate again, delete the reviewed entries, verify emptiness, and restore the intended rules using the latest settings revision. This affects visitors and is a separate intentional maintenance action; pausing writes does not identify which entries are tests.
6. **Change an application setting:** if it is an application value (theme, event date), patch the appropriate entry in a declared settings collection. If it governs visitor permissions, update collection rules. If it is a title/description/favicon, edit `index.html`.
7. **Remove content:** delete a known file ID for one file, or call `trash_site` with the site lifecycle revision for the entire site. Report the 30-day recovery deadline and browser app URL after Trash; never describe it as immediate permanent deletion.

Concurrent conflicts are expected, not transient errors to bypass. Bound automatic reconciliation attempts (proposed maximum three); if intent cannot be preserved, return the unresolved IDs and current state for later work. Never overwrite a visitor's newer value just to finish an unattended run.

## Errors and implementation acceptance checks

Tool execution failures return `isError: true` and a structured `Failure`. Protocol validation/authentication failures use the supported MCP protocol/auth path. Stable product codes include `NOT_FOUND`, `FORBIDDEN`, `SITE_TRASHED`, `ALREADY_EXISTS`, `REVISION_CONFLICT`, `EDIT_MATCH_FAILED`, `INVALID_ARGUMENT`, `COLLECTION_NOT_DECLARED`, `LIMIT_EXCEEDED`, `RATE_LIMITED`, `CURSOR_EXPIRED`, and `REQUEST_ID_REUSED`. Do not leak inaccessible resource existence or saved values in errors. Include current revisions only when the caller may read that resource. Supply retry timing for rate limits and actionable next steps; raw database/provider errors stay internal.

Verify these properties when implementing the tools:

- Two writers using one entry revision: exactly one mutation succeeds; the loser preserves the winner's value. Include visitor-vs-agent patch and delete races.
- File edits, rule changes, and HTML replacement leave saved entries intact. A wrong-scope ID or revision is refused.
- Retried create/delete/Trash calls return one durable effect. Test a lost response, deduplication expiry, and changed input under a reused request ID.
- Invalid or ambiguous text edits have no partial effects. Missing required targets cannot widen deletion scope or turn updates into creates.
- Cross-workspace calls and capabilities missing from otherwise valid tokens are denied. Rule revocation affects live subscriptions and in-flight visitor writes.
- `index.html` alone publishes; derived metadata follows HTML changes. Trash stops serving and visitor writes, retains contents, and restoration remains browser-only.

## Assumptions made while the owner is away

- **Publication:** writes are immediately live, with no draft, release, or explicit deploy stage. An empty site has a reserved URL but serves no placeholder. Multi-file atomic deployment is deferred.
- **Data model:** saved data consists of named collections of JSON-object entries. The brief specifies entry-sized access but not collection names, field schemas, keys, or query syntax. No general query language, batch mutation, full-store replacement, or schema migration engine is needed initially.
- **Concurrency:** all entry writers, including the visitor SDK, use optimistic revision checks. Conflicts preserve current data rather than invoking last-write-wins behavior. Entry deletions are immediate; the brief promises Trash for sites only.
- **Permissions:** explicit per-collection read/create/update/delete rules, default denial, server-assigned visitor identities, and separately authorized management access are the initial model. Anonymous identity recovery and more granular field/value validation need later product decisions.
- **Settings:** policy settings are stored separately from application data and HTML metadata. Collection-rule changes add or update one declaration; there is no declaration-deletion tool initially. Setting visitor rules to owner-only retains the declaration and entries.
- **Bounds:** numeric payload, page, cursor, reconciliation, and deduplication bounds above are initial design choices. Aggregate site limits and commercial quotas remain undecided and must be supplied by the product limit contract before implementation.
- **Workspace access:** discovery lists only workspaces granted by the authenticated connection. A grant may cover several workspaces; a connection constrained to one lists only that one. Never use an arbitrary input workspace ID as proof of membership. Workspace creation, membership administration, billing, custom domains, browser automation, and asset generation remain outside this catalog.
- **Transport:** this document defines product tools, not an MCP transport/version or OAuth implementation. Select and verify those contracts during implementation; no new external service or provider choice is implied here.
